WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.440
A barley leaf cannot take in CO2 without letting water out.

2
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.780
The gas enters through tiny pores called stomata.

3
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.600
Water vapour leaves through the very same pores.

4
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.960
When the stomata are open, both things happen at once.

5
00:00:21.783 --> 00:00:25.943
An adult human lung faces a version of the same bargain.

6
00:00:27.043 --> 00:00:32.083
Gas must dissolve in a thin, wet film before it can diffuse into cells.

7
00:00:33.003 --> 00:00:36.663
A wet surface in air loses water by evaporation.

8
00:00:37.563 --> 00:00:39.703
Every breath carries water away.

9
00:00:40.883 --> 00:00:42.663
A stoma can close its gap.

10
00:00:43.563 --> 00:00:46.883
The lung sits inside the chest and cannot close.

11
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:49.917
How do we count the cost?

12
00:00:51.237 --> 00:00:53.797
Divide the carbon gained by the water lost.

13
00:00:54.597 --> 00:00:56.797
This is called water-use efficiency.

14
00:00:57.897 --> 00:01:00.817
Here are two made-up leaves, not barley data.

15
00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:08.257
Leaf A gains 20 micromoles of CO2 and loses 5 millimoles of water per square metre

16
00:01:08.257 --> 00:01:14.757
per second. Its efficiency is 4.0 micromoles of CO2 per millimole of water.

17
00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:18.207
Leaf B has fewer stomata.

18
00:01:18.847 --> 00:01:24.247
It gains 16 and loses 3, so its efficiency is 5.3.

19
00:01:25.107 --> 00:01:29.807
It gains 20% less carbon, but loses 40% less water.

20
00:01:31.027 --> 00:01:32.867
Neither leaf is better everywhere.

21
00:01:33.847 --> 00:01:38.507
When water is scarce, leaf B keeps more water for each unit of carbon.

22
00:01:39.507 --> 00:01:44.067
When water is plentiful and carbon limits growth, leaf A gains more.

23
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.080
You might expect less grain from a leaf with fewer stomata.

24
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.620
Researchers tested this in barley.

25
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:57.160
They added extra copies of one barley gene, which limits stomatal development.

26
00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:04.720
In the mature sixth leaf, two lines kept about 24% and 12% of the control stomatal

27
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.540
density. Gas exchange fell in well-watered plants.

28
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.240
With five plants per group, no statistically significant drop in grain yield was

29
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.220
seen. That does not prove the yields equal.

30
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.420
It means no loss was detected.

31
00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.790
Why does a structure usually fit what it does?

32
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:27.170
Selection acts on inherited variation.

33
00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:33.630
A sound claim is that lineages with fewer stomata lost less water and left more

34
00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.190
descendants in dry places.

35
00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:39.410
It is not that the plant made fewer stomata to save water.

36
00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:44.390
And the fit is never perfect, because a trade-off links two traits.

37
00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.070
Improving one worsens the other.

38
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.390
Every exchange surface carries a cost.
